sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch09-14> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Since no oceanic island bears Palaeozoic or Secondary formations, oceans have probably occupied their present areas, and continents theirs, since the earliest Silurian period, though the arrangement may have differed long before.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Oceans and continents have kept roughly their present positions since the Silurian";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IX, “On the Imperfection of the Geological Record”; section “On their sudden appearance in the lowest known fossiliferous strata”. Scope: global distribution of oceans and continents since the Silurian"
}
